# Architectural Constitution

These 3 laws are MANDATORY for all code changes. Violating them = PR rejected.

## LAW 1: ONE OWNER RULE

Every state domain has exactly ONE writable owner. All others are read-only.

| Domain | Owner (WRITE) | Others (READ-ONLY) |
|---|---|---|
| router config | RouterConfigManager | CONFIG (readonly), ConfigService (delegator) |
| circuit breaker | KeyStateStore | adapter (read), CrossTab (sync) |
| health data | KeyStateStore | HealthService (write-through), ProviderTracker (read) |
| SLA mode | SettingsService | Kernel (read), CONFIG (delete) |
| fallback chains | SettingsService | RoutingPolicy (read on event) |
| pricing | SettingsService | llm.pricing (read), fallbackPricing (delete) |
| memory entries | MemoryService | Worker (delegated), MemoryContextPanel (read) |
| key state | KeyStateStore | KeyStateProjection (read), Registry (delegated) |
| chat sessions | Dexie via useChatStore | localStorage dump (DELETE) |
| debate sessions | DebateRuntime | legacy DebateService (DELETE) |
| debate persistence | DexieDebateStore | localStorage session/history (DELETE) |
| event log | EventRecorder | RingEventLog (DELETE) |
| kernel state | SystemKernel via DAL | Dexie KV (write-through), localStorage (DELETE) |
| cache | CacheService via DAL | Dexie KV (write-through) |
| budget | BudgetService via DAL | Dexie KV (write-through) |

BEFORE any PR:
1. Does the changed domain have one owner? If no = reject.
2. Does anyone else write to the same domain? If yes = Law 1 violation.

## LAW 2: NO PARALLEL WRITES

It is FORBIDDEN to write the same data to two stores simultaneously.

FORBIDDEN:
- localStorage + Dexie for same domain
- old service + new service for same domain
- 2 Zustand stores for same domain
- in-memory Map + persistent store without write-through
- 2 EventBus instances emitting the same event

ALLOWED:
- write-through: service -> Dexie (primary), in-memory cache updates AFTER confirmation
- read-through: in-memory cache -> Dexie (on cache miss)
- dual-read during migration (read both, take freshest)
- BroadcastChannel for cross-tab INVALIDATION (not data writes)

Migration protocol (the ONLY allowed dual-write):
1. Phase dual-read: New owner reads. Old still writes. Both stores read, take freshest.
2. Phase switch-write: New owner starts writing. Old stops.
3. Phase verify: Confirm new owner survives reload.
4. Phase cleanup: Delete old store and old writer.

Each phase = one sprint. Not infinite.

## LAW 3: DEPRECATION ENFORCEMENT

Old code does not live forever as fallback. It has a lifespan.

Service lifecycle:
- ACTIVE: Used in production, receives features
- DEPRECATED: Replaced, still imported. Has @deprecated JSDoc + console.warn()
- FROZEN: Not imported by anyone, file still exists. Don't touch.
- DEAD: File deleted. git history is the only place.

Timeline:
- ACTIVE -> DEPRECATED: When new owner is assigned
- DEPRECATED -> FROZEN: 1 sprint after all consumers switched
- FROZEN -> DEAD: 1 sprint after confirming zero imports

BEFORE any PR:
1. Does the PR import anything with FROZEN or DEAD status? If yes = reject.
2. Does the PR add a new file without an owner? If yes = reject.

## MIGRATION LOCK MODE

Until consolidation is complete, it is FORBIDDEN to:
- Add new storage technologies
- Create new singletons with mutable state
- Add new localStorage keys
- Create new EventBus instances
- Implement new panels without a data owner

Only allowed changes: bugfixes + migrations + dead code removal.
